// Where the rows a pass selected become the structure a briefing
// keeps. The node above answered one item holding the findings this
// digest covers, the deliveries the domain has standing and the
// failures of the run that closed before this one; this one answers
// what a period came to, as one assembled value, and nothing below
// re-derives any part of what it decided.
//
// TWO LIBRARIES, ONE NODE, which is the only place a spliced library
// can meet a sibling at all. src/lib/digest-assemble.ts decides
// everything about a period that is a function of the rows: the
// order the findings go in, which section each is filed under, how
// many each section holds, and what the pass before this one could
// not do. src/lib/sanitize-md.ts bounds what the untrusted text
// inside them can DO. Neither states the other's rule, the two share
// no top-level name over both sources, and the order they are
// spliced in is free rather than load-bearing.
//
// THE ORDER IS REDUCE THEN ASSEMBLE, which is the opposite of
// ar-research's record node and is the opposite for a reason. There
// is nothing to judge here: every value arrived from this domain's
// own tables rather than from a model, so no rule is applied to text
// that a reduction would then have edited out from under it. What
// reducing first buys is that the assembly's own fallbacks act on
// the text a reader will actually see, so a category label whose
// whole content reduces to nothing is headed by its key and an alias
// that reduces to nothing takes the neutral word, rather than either
// heading being the punctuation around an empty value. It also keeps
// what was stored and what was matched the same string, the section
// a finding is filed under being decided on the reduced value.
//
// A 0 IS A READING AND A null IS NOT. A section that was read and
// held nothing is 0, and a section nothing was read for is null: the
// first says the domain looked and this bucket was empty, the second
// says nobody looked, and they are different facts about a period
// that a renderer shows differently. A 0 written for an unread
// section is the unscored finding written as zero one level up, a
// measurement claim about something nothing measured, and it is
// quieter still than that one, because a count is what a heading
// carries and a reader takes a printed (0) to mean the domain
// looked.
//
// WHERE THAT DIFFERENCE IS MADE ON THIS CANVAS is the coverage claim
// below and nowhere else. It is the one thing about the counts that
// is not derivable from the findings, since a pass that read every
// section and a pass that read one hand the assembly the same list
// whenever the other sections were empty. Select Digest Material
// selects every finding of the domain inside the window with no
// category predicate anywhere in it, so when that statement ran,
// every section was read: each key the domain declared, and the one
// section nobody declares. When the pass resolved no domain the
// statement selected against nothing, no section was read, and every
// count is null along with the total. The library overrides the
// claim in one direction only, counting a section that holds
// findings whatever was claimed, because the findings are evidence
// and the claim is an assertion.
//
// THE ASSEMBLY IS WHAT BOTH SIDES ARE HELD TO. What this node
// answers is what the write node stores in briefings.payload, and
// the stored payload is what every renderer under src/exports/ reads
// back: src/exports/markdown-body.ts lays out the sections and their
// counts as this assembly fixed them and could not re-derive one if
// it wanted to, ExportFindingRow carrying no category key at all, so
// the rows a renderer is handed are ordered and unsectioned. The
// ordering, the sectioning, the headings and the counts are
// therefore decided once, here, which is what keeps four formats
// from disagreeing about what a period came to. A briefing carrying
// no assembly is still legitimate, one written by hand or backfilled
// having nothing structured in it, and composes to its prose alone.
//
// WHERE A FINDING'S SECTION COMES FROM. findings carries no category
// column: a domain files a finding under one of its categories
// through the fields payload its own DomainSettings.fieldContract
// governs, so the association is a member of a payload rather than a
// column, and this node is the projection digest-assemble.ts says
// its caller makes. The member name is the constant below. A domain
// whose contract names it something else, or names nothing like it
// at all, files every finding in the section nobody declares, which
// is visible in its own digest under a heading rather than silent,
// and loses no finding: an unrecognised key files there too, which
// is the same answer reached from the other side.
//
// WHAT IS REDUCED AND WHAT IS NOT. Reduced: the fields payload,
// whole and at every depth, keys as well as values; the previous
// run's errors in whatever shape that column carried them; and the
// two operator-authored labels that land inside a heading, a
// category's name and the domain's display alias. Not reduced: the
// scores, the ids and the stamps, which have no active form and
// whose spelling an ordering depends on, and a category KEY, which
// is the identity a renderer matches a section on and what the
// taxonomy is joined by, so a reduced one would name a section
// categories does not hold. A key is not untrusted text either: it
// is an operator's own configuration, which is the reading
// declaredKeys makes of it when it names one in a refusal.
//
// THE WALK IS ITERATIVE, over an explicit stack rather than by
// recursion. A fields payload is stored jsonb and nothing bounds how
// deep one nests, so a recursive walk turns a deep but storable
// payload into a RangeError and a whole pass into nothing while
// every fixture-sized case stays green. Members are written with
// defineProperty for the reason any rebuild of a stored record
// needs it: a key spelling __proto__ arrives as an own key out of
// JSON.parse, and plain assignment would set the prototype instead
// of storing anything.
//
// NOTHING HERE REFUSES A PASS THAT RESOLVED NOTHING, which parts
// this node from ar-score's own assembly node. A digest pass with no
// domain has nothing to select and nothing to draft, and a refusal
// here would end the workflow with the runs row the dispatcher
// opened still waiting to be closed. What it answers instead is an
// assembly saying that nothing was read.
//
// CARDINALITY. One item in and one item out. The node above answers
// exactly one row by construction, every member of that statement
// being a scalar subquery over a SELECT naming no FROM, and the
// write node below needs it that way, briefings taking one row per
// pass rather than one per finding.

// The member of a finding's fields payload naming the category it is
// filed under. The header says what a domain naming it otherwise
// gets, and why that is visible rather than silent.
const FINDING_CATEGORY_FIELD = 'category';

// The domain row, its categories and its display settings, read back
// off the node that fetched them. Select Digest Material replaced the
// context item with its own answer, so a name is the only handle left
// on it.
const context = $('Load Domain Context').first().json;
const domain = context.domain;

// Whether this pass resolved the domain the run was opened for, and
// the whole of the coverage claim below. Load Domain Context answers
// a null domain for a hand-over naming no run and for a run naming no
// row, and the selection answers empty lists in both, so this is what
// separates a pass that read every section and found some of them
// empty from a pass that read no section at all.
const resolved = domain !== null && domain !== undefined;

// A value a walk descends into. The JSON null literal is not one,
// typeof answering object for it, and neither is anything scalar.
function isBranch(value) {
  return typeof value === 'object' && value !== null;
}

// A plain keyed object, or an empty one. A jsonb array and the JSON
// null literal both answer object to typeof and neither is a record a
// reader can take members off.
function objectOrEmpty(value) {
  return isBranch(value) && !Array.isArray(value)
    ? value
    : {};
}

// A list, or an empty one, for the reason asList in the spliced
// library gives: a declared type is a claim about the query rather
// than a guarantee about what arrived.
function listOrEmpty(value) {
  return Array.isArray(value)
    ? value
    : [];
}

// One member written as its own key rather than as an instruction to
// the object holding it. See the header.
function putMember(into, name, value) {
  Object.defineProperty(into, name, {
    configurable: true,
    enumerable: true,
    value,
    writable: true,
  });
}

// Every string inside a stored value, reduced, with the value's own
// shape rebuilt around it. A scalar answers itself, so a number, a
// boolean and a null cross unchanged and a list stays a list, which
// is what keeps previousRunBanner's reading of the errors column a
// reading of what was stored rather than of what this walk made of
// it.
//
// Two keys of one object that reduce to one name collapse onto the
// later of them. That is the same answer a document showing both
// under one heading would give, and it costs a field an operator's
// contract never declared, the reduction leaving an ordinary key as
// it stands.
function reduceDeep(value) {
  if (typeof value === 'string') {
    return sanitizeUntrusted(value);
  }

  if (!isBranch(value)) {
    return value;
  }

  const root = Array.isArray(value)
    ? []
    : {};
  const tasks = [{ from: value, into: root }];

  while (tasks.length > 0) {
    const task = tasks.pop();
    const indexed = Array.isArray(task.from);

    for (const key of Object.keys(task.from)) {
      const held = task.from[key];
      const name = indexed
        ? key
        : sanitizeUntrusted(key);

      if (isBranch(held)) {
        const branch = Array.isArray(held)
          ? []
          : {};

        tasks.push({ from: held, into: branch });
        putMember(task.into, name, branch);
      } else {
        putMember(task.into, name, typeof held === 'string'
          ? sanitizeUntrusted(held)
          : held);
      }
    }
  }

  return root;
}

// The category key a finding names, read off its reduced fields
// payload by own key alone: the payload came out of a column rather
// than out of this body, so it carries a prototype, and an inherited
// member standing in for one a domain never wrote would file findings
// under a section on the strength of nothing.
//
// A member that is not a string names no category, which files the
// finding in the section nobody declares. So does a key this domain
// does not declare, which the library decides rather than this node.
function categoryKeyOf(fields) {
  const named = Object.hasOwn(fields, FINDING_CATEGORY_FIELD)
    ? fields[FINDING_CATEGORY_FIELD]
    : null;

  return typeof named === 'string'
    ? named
    : null;
}

// One finding, in the spelling a renderer's own row shape uses, with
// its fields reduced and the section it was filed under recorded
// beside it.
//
// The eight members are the eight ExportFindingRow names, which is
// what the selection above chose to project, so a finding a stored
// briefing carries and a row a renderer is handed later are one shape
// rather than two that can drift. The ninth is the filing decision
// itself, and it can legitimately differ from the section this
// finding ends up in: a key this domain does not declare files under
// the section nobody declares, and keeping the key is what lets a
// stored briefing say why a finding is where it is.
//
// Everything else the row carried stops here. The library sections
// and orders whatever it is handed and carries the objects through
// untouched, so a member added to the projection is a member every
// stored briefing then holds.
function assembleFinding(row) {
  const fields = reduceDeep(objectOrEmpty(row.fields));

  return {
    id: row.id,
    domainId: row.domain_id,
    documentId: row.document_id,
    entityId: row.entity_id,
    fields,
    score: row.score,
    scoreVersion: row.score_version,
    createdAt: row.created_at,
    categoryKey: categoryKeyOf(fields),
  };
}

// The domain's categories as the sections of this digest, in the
// order the taxonomy query answered them. The key is carried as it
// stands and the label is the reduced name, which is the column
// behind what the library calls a label.
//
// A category naming no usable key, and two of them naming one
// section, are refused by the library rather than dropped here. The
// refusal is unreachable from this selection, categories.key being
// NOT NULL and categories_domain_id_key_unique forbidding the pair,
// so reaching it would mean the taxonomy query had stopped meaning
// what it means.
const categories = listOrEmpty(context.categories).map((row) => ({
  key: row.key,
  label: typeof row.name === 'string'
    ? sanitizeUntrusted(row.name)
    : null,
}));

// The heading vocabulary, reduced the same way and for the same
// reason. displayNameFor reads a value that is not a string, and one
// whose whole content is whitespace, as no alias at all, so an alias
// reduced to nothing takes the neutral word here rather than heading
// a section with an empty string.
const declaredAlias = objectOrEmpty(objectOrEmpty(domain).settings)
  .findingsDisplayName;
const settings = {
  findingsDisplayName: typeof declaredAlias === 'string'
    ? sanitizeUntrusted(declaredAlias)
    : '',
};

// The one item the selection answered. A node handed no item does not
// run, so this is a backstop rather than a path: what it stops is a
// selection above this one that has stopped answering exactly one
// row.
const carried = $input.first();

if (carried === undefined) {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-digest] Assemble Digest was handed no item: the selection '
    + 'above answers exactly one row and this pass reached it with '
    + 'none',
  );
}

const selected = carried.json;

// What this pass read, which the header argues is every section or no
// section and never a subset.
const sectionsRead = resolved
  ? [...categories.map((section) => section.key), null]
  : [];

const payload = assembleDigest({
  findings: listOrEmpty(selected.findings).map(assembleFinding),
  categories,
  sectionsRead,
  settings,
  previousErrors: reduceDeep(selected.previous_errors),
});

// The run and the domain travel because the ledger row, the stored
// briefing and the closing update are all charged to them. What this
// pass was dispatched for does not: the subscriptions the selection
// read stay one node reference away rather than being restated here,
// and a second copy of a fact is a second thing that can disagree.
// The counts and the total are inside the assembly for the same
// reason, that value being the one authority on what the period came
// to.
return [{
  json: {
    run_id: selected.run_id,
    domain_id: selected.domain_id,
    briefing_payload: payload,
  },
}];

// Where a digest pass decides how much it is going to spend, and the
// first node on this canvas that decides anything about money.
// Nothing above it opens a connection to a model and nothing below
// it can reduce what this one lets through. The ceiling is applied
// here, before a chunk is prepared, because preparing one is work
// and the cheapest call is the one that was never staged.
//
// THE CEILING IS A DECLARED CONSTANT. MAX_DRAFT_CALLS_PER_RUN below
// is an integer written into this source, which is what
// docs/architecture/01-invariants.md means by an explicit per run
// ceiling: a bound a reviewer finds by reading the declarations
// rather than by following the arithmetic. It is deliberately not a
// settings marker, on the reasoning ar-ingest gives at the node of
// this name. ENV_DEFAULTS in scripts/workflow-markers.ts is where a
// value an operator tunes per deployment lives, and moving this one
// there is that edit plus its own entry in that table rather than
// something to imply here.
//
// IT IS ONE, AND ONE IS THE WHOLE POINT OF DECLARING IT. A period
// comes to one digest: the node above assembles a single value out
// of every finding the window held, and the prose half of a briefing
// is one body drafted from that value rather than one body per
// section or per finding. So the slice below drops nothing today,
// and saying that is not an argument against it. What it stops is
// exactly the change that would go unnoticed otherwise -- a
// selection or an assembly that started answering a row per section,
// or per subscription, turning one drafting call per tick into as
// many as the domain has buckets. The two siblings bound a batch
// that is already plural; this bounds a batch that is meant to stay
// singular, and the bound is where that intent is written down.
//
// IT IS NOT THE PERIOD'S OWN BOUND. Select Digest Material bounds
// what a digest COVERS by the stamp on the newest stored briefing,
// and that is a statement about which findings belong to this
// period. This bounds what the pass may SPEND on saying what they
// came to. The two never trade against each other: a window holding
// four hundred findings and a window holding four are one call each.
//
// WHAT IS WORTH A CALL is read off the assembly and off nothing
// else. total is the count digest-assemble.ts derived from the
// sections it built, and its two absences are different facts about
// the period: null is a pass that read no section at all, which is
// the domain the run was opened for resolving to nothing, and 0 is a
// pass that read every section and found each of them empty. Neither
// gives a model anything to write about, so neither is staged, and
// the difference between them is kept where it was made rather than
// collapsed here.
//
// A PERIOD THAT CAME TO NOTHING IS NOT LOST BY NOT BEING DRAFTED.
// The banner, the section headings and the counts are the assembly's
// and are already decided; src/exports/markdown-body.ts lays out an
// empty digest from them and every renderer under src/exports/
// answers a briefing that came to nothing with its own preamble.
// What a drafting call would add is a sentence saying what the
// counts already say, once per tick, for as long as a domain stays
// quiet.
//
// WHAT AN UNSTAGED PASS COSTS IS REAL AND IS NOT THIS NODE'S TO PAY.
// A pass that stages nothing ends this chain here: no chunk is
// prepared, no call is made, no ledger row is written, and every
// node below runs on nothing. The group that stores the briefing and
// the group that closes the runs row the dispatcher opened both have
// to be reachable for such a pass, which is a wiring question for
// those groups rather than something this node can answer by
// emitting an item nobody could draft from. ar-ingest's Score
// Findings names the mechanism this canvas already uses for the same
// shape.
//
// THE CONNECTOR IS REFUSED RATHER THAN PASSED ON. A deployment with
// no llm connector, or one whose config states no endpoint, has
// nowhere to send a call, and this node stops the pass by name
// instead of emitting nothing. Emitting nothing is indistinguishable
// from a period that came to nothing, and a deployment in that state
// would go on storing digests with no prose in them quietly for as
// long as nobody looked.
//
// THAT IS NOT THE REFUSAL Assemble Digest DECLINED TO MAKE, and the
// two are worth telling apart because they sit two nodes from each
// other. That node answers an assembly saying nothing was read
// rather than raising, because a domain that resolved to nothing is
// a state a digest can report about itself. A missing endpoint is a
// deployment somebody has not finished configuring, it is identical
// on every pass until an operator acts, and no briefing this canvas
// could write would say so. What the refusal costs is the run row
// the group below would close, which is the same cost ar-ingest's
// node of this name names.
//
// CARDINALITY. This node is handed one item, the connector, and
// answers one for the period it stages or none at all. The assembly
// is read back by name, for the reason everything on this canvas is:
// the Postgres node in front of this one replaced the item that held
// it.

// One drafting call per pass. Written out rather than shared,
// because a SQL statement can splice nothing and no marker declares
// this number; the twenty five the rest of the set bounds its
// batches with is a different bound over a different thing and is
// not this one made smaller.
const MAX_DRAFT_CALLS_PER_RUN = 1;

// How many findings the period came to, as the assembly counted
// them, or null where it counted nothing. Read defensively for the
// reason every reading of a jsonb value on this canvas is: the
// payload crossed a Postgres node, and a member that is not a finite
// number is one nothing measured rather than a zero.
function findingTotal(payload) {
  if (payload === null || typeof payload !== 'object') {
    return null;
  }

  const total = payload.total;

  return typeof total === 'number' && Number.isFinite(total)
    ? total
    : null;
}

const connector = $input.first();

if (connector === undefined) {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-digest] Apply Call Ceiling was handed no connector item, '
    + 'which Select Model Connector answers whatever the table holds',
  );
}

const endpoint = connector.json.endpoint;

if (typeof endpoint !== 'string' || endpoint === '') {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-digest] Apply Call Ceiling has no endpoint to spend '
    + 'against: connectors holds no llm row whose config states one',
  );
}

// Staged in the order the assembly answered, which is one item on
// every pass this canvas can produce. Nothing here re-orders
// anything, there being nothing to order.
const staged = [];

for (const item of $('Assemble Digest').all()) {
  const row = item.json;
  const total = findingTotal(row.briefing_payload);

  if (total === null || total === 0) {
    continue;
  }

  staged.push(row);
}

// Sliced rather than filtered, so the bound is the one declared
// above and never a property of what happened to arrive. What is
// carried forward is the run and the domain a row below is written
// against and the assembled value the node below composes a chunk
// out of, which is everything the item held: this node drops members
// from no item, it drops whole items or none.
return staged.slice(0, MAX_DRAFT_CALLS_PER_RUN).map((row) => ({
  json: {
    run_id: row.run_id,
    domain_id: row.domain_id,
    briefing_payload: row.briefing_payload,
  },
}));

