// Where an assembled period becomes the one payload a model may
// receive. Both libraries above are spliced in whole and are wired
// together here, which is the only place they can meet:
// src/lib/chunk.ts reduces the material a digest is drafted from to
// a bounded chunk and refuses one that is not worth a call, and
// src/lib/prompt-frame.ts takes that chunk and fences it so that
// everything inside the fence is read as data and never as
// instructions. Nothing below states a reduction rule or a framing
// rule of its own.
//
// AND NOTHING ELSE GOES FORWARD, which is the whole reason this
// node exists as a separate one. The item it answers carries the two
// framed halves, the run and the domain a row below is written
// against, and the measurements of the prompt. The assembled value
// does not survive it, nor any finding's text, nor a section
// heading. So no expression below can reach for the stored material,
// and the model node is fed a prepared chunk because there is
// nothing else on the item to feed it.
//
// THE ASSEMBLY IS STILL THERE, one node reference away, and that is
// deliberate rather than a leak. The group that stores the briefing
// writes briefings.payload from $('Assemble Digest') and the prose
// from the call this node prepares, so the structured half never
// makes the round trip through a prompt and back, and what is stored
// is what the assembly decided rather than what a model read of it.
//
// THE TRUSTED HALF AND THE UNTRUSTED HALF ARRIVE AS TWO MEMBERS,
// which is the framing library's structural claim rather than a
// convenience. A caller wiring a model node has to decide which
// channel each half goes in, and two members make that decision
// visible on this canvas: the node below puts system in the system
// message and data in the prompt, and neither can be confused for
// the other by an expression that reached for one string.
//
// THE PERSONA IS A ROW AND NEVER A LITERAL. It arrives from the
// domain's personas, fetched by Load Domain Context at the top of
// this pass, and personas in src/db/schema/domains.ts argues at
// length why no prompt string is written into a workflow file at
// all: one file serves every domain, the text is what an operator
// tunes most, and a prompt fixed on a canvas is lost at the next
// import. The role read is the drafter, which is the third of the
// three the seed writes and the only one no other workflow frames
// against -- ar-ingest and ar-research both read the researcher, and
// ar-score makes no call at all. So this is the one role whose text
// is spent here and nowhere else.
//
// THERE IS A HEADER, AND EVERY LINE IN IT IS A FACT ABOUT THE ASK.
// buildChunk takes a roster of fields and the values already
// resolved for them, and writes a header line per field so a model
// does not have to guess what is known. Four are known before this
// call and none of them is a guess about the answer: which period is
// being written about, what this domain calls a finding, which
// sections it declares and in what order, and how many findings the
// period came to. The first is what makes a digest periodic, the
// second is the vocabulary the prose has to use, and the last two
// are what the structured half already says, offered so the prose
// and the structure agree rather than disagreeing about the same
// period.
//
// THE HEADER IS INSIDE THE FENCE, like every other part of a chunk,
// and that is right rather than a compromise. The values in it came
// from this deployment's own tables and are trusted, but a heading
// an operator typed is still text somebody wrote, and neutralizing
// it costs nothing that matters here: a period stamp, a count and a
// list of headings have no active form to lose.
//
// THE PERIOD IS THE BOUNDARY AND NOT A RANGE. briefings carries no
// period column, so what a stored digest covers is the span between
// its own stamp and its predecessor's, and the only end of that span
// this pass can state before writing its own row is the beginning.
// A domain with nothing stored has no beginning either, and the
// stand-in below says so rather than leaving the line off.
//
// THE ORDER THE BODY IS COMPOSED IN IS THE ASSEMBLY'S. Sections run
// in the order the domain declared its categories with the section
// under no category last, and the findings inside each run by score
// with an absent score behind every score there is. Neither is
// decided here. What that buys is more than tidiness once the
// excerpt ceiling below bites: what survives a truncation is the
// front of the digest, which is the sections a domain put first and
// the findings that scored highest within them.
//
// THE EXCERPT CEILING IS THE LIBRARY'S, IT IS SHARED, AND HERE IT
// BINDS HARDEST. MAX_EXCERPT_CHARS bounds the whole composed
// excerpt, so a period's every section and every finding in them
// share the bound one document has to itself at ingest. A domain
// with a busy period is drafted from the front of its own digest and
// not from all of it, and that is a real cost paid in prose
// coverage. What it is never paid in is structure: the counts, the
// sections and the findings a renderer lays out come from the stored
// assembly, which this node does not touch and truncation cannot
// reach.
//
// A DIGEST WITH NOTHING TO SAY IS NOT ASKED ABOUT. This node does
// not set allowFieldsOnly, and the omission is the decision: a
// header alone is a period stamp, a count and a list of headings,
// and a call made from one would be a call answered out of the
// model's own parameters and stored as this domain's account of a
// period. The node above already declines to stage a period whose
// findings came to none or to nothing read, so what a chunk refusal
// catches here is the narrower case that survives that -- findings
// whose fields hold no prose worth reading, or too little of it.
//
// TWO REFUSALS, and they are answered differently because they are
// faults of different things. A chunk refusal is about this period:
// the material behind it reduced to nothing, or to less than the
// library's minimum. That period is not drafted and the pass carries
// on with nothing on this output, which is the same shape the node
// above describes for a period it declined to stage. A persona
// refusal is about the operator's own text, is identical on every
// pass until somebody edits a row, and would otherwise be a
// deployment that quietly stored digests with no prose in them, so
// it stops the pass by name. Which of the three the framing library
// refused is what parts them, and CHUNK_EMPTY_REASON is the one that
// belongs to the period.
//
// THAT PARTS THIS NODE FROM ar-research'S, where a chunk refusal
// skips one candidate out of a batch and the pass goes on to the
// next. There is no next here. A period is one item, so its refusal
// is the pass's, which is why the reason is worth reading rather
// than assuming: the two faults look identical from the far end of
// an empty output.
//
// THE TEXT WAS ALREADY REDUCED AND IS REDUCED AGAIN. Assemble Digest
// put every fields payload through sanitize-md.ts before the
// assembly saw it, which is what bounds what the stored value can
// DO, and promptFrame neutralizes the composed chunk again on its
// way into the fence, which is what bounds what a model reads it as.
// The two are different rules over the same text and neither stands
// in for the other, so neither is skipped here on the strength of
// the other having run.
//
// CARDINALITY. One item in, at most one out, and none whenever a
// chunk was refused. Nothing below pairs against the node above this
// one: the ledger below pairs against THIS node by position, which
// on this canvas is a single position by construction.

// The role whose system text this workflow speaks with.
// personas.role is free text and carries no CHECK, the roles a
// pipeline plays growing with the pipeline, so this is a lookup into
// rows an operator wrote rather than a member of a closed set. The
// seed writes three and this is the one nothing else reads.
const PERSONA_ROLE = 'drafter';

// The header roster, in the order buildChunk writes it. No entry
// carries an identifying flag, and the omission is deliberate: that
// flag governs allowFieldsOnly alone, which this node never sets, so
// writing one would be configuration nothing reads.
const DIGEST_FIELDS = [
  {
    key: 'period',
    label: 'Period covered, from',
    fallback: '(the first digest for this domain, covering everything)',
  },
  {
    key: 'vocabulary',
    label: 'What this domain calls a finding',
    fallback: '(the assembly names no word)',
  },
  {
    key: 'sections',
    label: 'Sections, in order',
    fallback: '(the assembly declares no sections)',
  },
  {
    key: 'total',
    label: 'Findings in this period',
    fallback: '(the assembly counted none)',
  },
];

// What separates the members of a list inside one header line.
// buildChunk collapses every value to a single line, so this is
// about what a reader and a model can both parse rather than about
// layout.
const HEADING_JOIN = ', ';

// What separates one composed block from the next in the body, and
// what separates the lines inside one. collapseWhitespace keeps
// paragraph structure and collapses nothing shorter, so both survive
// the reduction as written.
const BLOCK_JOIN = '\n\n';
const ENTRY_JOIN = '\n';

// What stands between a field's own name and what it holds.
const FIELD_JOIN = ': ';

// A stored value as one line of prose, or the empty string where
// there is nothing to write. A string is taken as it stands and a
// finite number is spelled out, because a fields payload legitimately
// holds both and a count somebody stored is as much a fact about a
// finding as its title. Anything else is dropped rather than coerced:
// an object or a list rendered through String is a shape rather than
// a sentence, and a model reading one learns nothing it could cite.
function readValue(value) {
  if (typeof value === 'string') {
    return value.trim();
  }

  return typeof value === 'number' && Number.isFinite(value)
    ? String(value)
    : '';
}

// One finding's fields, as one line each, in the order the payload
// holds them. Own keys only, so a payload spelling a prototype
// member cannot put a function's text into a prompt, and a key whose
// value reads as nothing takes no line at all rather than a labelled
// blank.
function fieldLines(fields) {
  const lines = [];

  if (fields === null || typeof fields !== 'object') {
    return lines;
  }

  for (const key of Object.keys(fields)) {
    const text = readValue(fields[key]);

    if (text !== '') {
      lines.push(key + FIELD_JOIN + text);
    }
  }

  return lines;
}

// How one finding is introduced in the composed body. The marker is
// trusted text this node writes and everything after it is somebody
// else's, which is the same shape ar-research uses for a document.
//
// The score rides on the marker because it is what the ordering
// around it was decided by, and it obeys the law it was stored
// under: a measured score is written and an absent one says it is
// absent, rather than a zero standing in for a reading nobody took.
function findingBlock(finding) {
  const scored = typeof finding.score === 'number'
    && Number.isFinite(finding.score);
  const said = scored
    ? ', score ' + String(finding.score)
    : ', unscored';
  const marker = '[finding ' + String(finding.id) + said + ']';

  return [marker, ...fieldLines(finding.fields)].join(ENTRY_JOIN);
}

// One section, as its heading and the findings under it. A section
// holding nothing contributes nothing at all rather than a heading
// over a blank: the counts are what say a bucket was read and empty,
// and they are in the stored assembly rather than in this prompt.
//
// The heading is the assembly's, which is a category's own label, or
// its key where the label reduced to nothing, or this domain's word
// for a finding for the one section no domain declares. Nothing here
// re-derives any of that.
function sectionBlock(section) {
  const findings = Array.isArray(section.findings)
    ? section.findings
    : [];

  if (findings.length === 0) {
    return '';
  }

  const heading = typeof section.heading === 'string'
    ? section.heading
    : '';
  const blocks = findings
    .filter((finding) => finding !== null && typeof finding === 'object')
    .map(findingBlock);

  return ['[section ' + heading + ']', ...blocks].join(BLOCK_JOIN);
}

// The sections of one assembly, in the order it fixed them.
function sectionsOf(payload) {
  if (payload === null || typeof payload !== 'object') {
    return [];
  }

  return Array.isArray(payload.sections)
    ? payload.sections.filter((s) => s !== null && typeof s === 'object')
    : [];
}

// The system text of the persona holding a role, or the empty string
// where there is none. Every member is typed on the way out, because
// these entries came back through jsonb and nothing has validated
// them: a row whose system_text is not text is a row an operator has
// not finished, and it refuses below with the same sentence a
// missing row does.
function personaSystemText(entries, role) {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (entry !== null && typeof entry === 'object' && entry.role === role
      && typeof entry.system_text === 'string') {
      return entry.system_text;
    }
  }

  return '';
}

const context = $('Load Domain Context').first().json;
const personas = Array.isArray(context.personas)
  ? context.personas
  : [];
const persona = personaSystemText(personas, PERSONA_ROLE);

// A pass with no drafter persona, or one whose text is not text.
// Both are the same fault from here and both are pass wide, so this
// refuses before a chunk is built rather than after.
if (persona === '') {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-digest] Prepare Model Prompt has no system text for the '
    + PERSONA_ROLE + ' persona: the domain states none, or the row '
    + 'it states holds no text',
  );
}

// The boundary this digest covers from, read off the node that
// selected the material rather than off the item: the assembly
// replaced that row and the stamp is not one of the three members it
// carried forward. A null stamp is a domain with nothing stored,
// which the roster's stand-in states.
const selected = $('Select Digest Material').first().json;
const since = typeof selected.since === 'string'
  ? selected.since
  : '';

const items = [];

for (const item of $input.all()) {
  const row = item.json;
  const payload = row.briefing_payload;
  const sections = sectionsOf(payload);
  const headings = sections
    .map((section) => (typeof section.heading === 'string'
      ? section.heading
      : ''))
    .filter((heading) => heading !== '');
  const total = payload !== null && typeof payload === 'object'
    ? payload.total
    : null;
  const built = buildChunk({
    fields: DIGEST_FIELDS,
    values: {
      period: since,
      vocabulary: payload !== null && typeof payload === 'object'
        ? payload.displayName
        : '',
      sections: headings.join(HEADING_JOIN),
      total: typeof total === 'number' && Number.isFinite(total)
        ? String(total)
        : '',
    },
    body: sections
      .map(sectionBlock)
      .filter((block) => block !== '')
      .join(BLOCK_JOIN),
  });

  if (!built.usable) {
    continue;
  }

  const framed = promptFrame(persona, built.chunk);

  if (!framed.usable) {
    // A framing refusal that is not about the chunk is about the
    // persona, and a persona fault repeats on every pass until
    // somebody edits the row. Refusing by name here is what parts
    // an operator text fault from a period with nothing readable
    // behind it.
    if (framed.reason !== CHUNK_EMPTY_REASON) {
      throw new Error(
        '[ar-digest] Prepare Model Prompt cannot frame the '
        + PERSONA_ROLE + ' persona: ' + framed.reason,
      );
    }

    continue;
  }

  const promptChars = framed.system.length + framed.data.length;

  // What the model node is fed, and what the two nodes after it
  // read. No section, no heading, no finding and no assembled value
  // is here: the fenced form is the only spelling of any of them
  // that leaves this node, and the assembly itself stays where it
  // was decided.
  items.push({
    json: {
      run_id: row.run_id,
      domain_id: row.domain_id,
      system: framed.system,
      data: framed.data,
      prompt_chars: promptChars,
      est_tokens: estimateTokens(promptChars),
      fence_cuts: framed.fenceCuts,
      forms_defanged: framed.formsDefanged,
    },
  });
}

return items;
